+section('git', 'Git repository integration', 'git')
  p.
    #[code --git-repo] makes the Client clone a repository before the job runs. #[code --command] then runs inside the checkout
    (#[code $THUB_WORK_DIR]). Typically the repository holds the test scripts. The firmware comes as a #[code --download-file]
    or is built by the command.

  h3.h6 1. Create a test repository
  +code('Your machine').
    mkdir firmware-tests && cd firmware-tests && git init -b main
    mkdir -p ci
    cat > ci/test.sh &lt;&lt;'EOF'
    #!/bin/sh
    set -eu
    echo "job $THUB_JOB_ID, suite $THUB_SUITE, commit $THUB_GIT_COMMIT"
    mkdir -p results
    pytest --junitxml=results/junit.xml tests/     # results/*.xml is uploaded and summarized
    EOF
    chmod +x ci/test.sh
    git add . && git commit -m "TestHub entry point"
    git remote add origin git@github.com:yourorg/firmware-tests.git
    git push -u origin main
  p.small.
    The exit code of #[code --command] is the verdict. JUnit files under #[code results/] or #[code artifacts/] are summed into
    the job's test counts. Nothing is uploaded: the workspace is deleted when the job ends, so publish anything you need to keep
    from the command itself (e.g. #[code curl -T report.html "$ARTIFACTORY/…"] with a token passed as #[code --env]).

  h3.h6 2. Give Clients read access: SSH deploy key (recommended)
  p.small.
    git never prompts on a Client (#[code GIT_TERMINAL_PROMPT=0]), so credentials must already be on the Client host,
    owned by the #[strong Client's service user]. Create a key on each Client host (or one shared key per lab):
  +code('Client host, as the Client\'s user').
    ssh-keygen -t ed25519 -N "" -C "thub-client@$(hostname)" -f ~/.ssh/thub_deploy
    cat ~/.ssh/thub_deploy.pub          # → add as a read-only deploy key (below)
    # trust the git server's host key now: the service can't answer prompts
    # (and can't write ~/.ssh under systemd)
    ssh-keyscan github.com bitbucket.org gitlab.com >> ~/.ssh/known_hosts
    # use the key for that server
    cat >> ~/.ssh/config &lt;&lt;'EOF'
    Host github.com
      IdentityFile ~/.ssh/thub_deploy
      IdentitiesOnly yes
    EOF
    chmod 600 ~/.ssh/config
    ssh -T git@github.com                # "…successfully authenticated…"
  table.table.table-sm.small
    thead
      tr
        th Git server
        th Where to add the public key
    tbody
      tr
        td GitHub
        td Repository → Settings → Deploy keys → Add deploy key (leave “Allow write access” off). For many repositories, use a machine user's SSH key instead.
      tr
        td GitLab
        td Project → Settings → Repository → Deploy keys.
      tr
        td Bitbucket
        td Repository settings → Security → Access keys.
      tr
        td Gitea / self-hosted
        td Repository → Settings → Deploy Keys, or the machine user's SSH keys.
  p.small.
    Without #[code ~/.ssh/config], pick the key per job with #[code --git-options] (stored with the job, so reference a key
    #[em file], never inline a secret):
  +code('Per-job SSH key / port').
    thub run --type sw \
      --git-repo ssh://git@git.lab.local:2222/qa/tests.git main \
      --git-options '-c core.sshCommand="ssh -i ~/.ssh/thub_deploy -o IdentitiesOnly=yes"' \
      --command ./ci/test.sh --wait

  h3.h6 HTTPS with a token
  p.small.
    For #[code https://] repositories, either store a credential helper for the Client's user on the host
    (#[code git config --global credential.helper store]), or pass the token #[strong per job] as a secret. #[code --env]
    variables reach the Client's own git commands, and git reads config from #[code GIT_CONFIG_COUNT]/#[code _KEY_n]/#[code _VALUE_n]:
  +code('Token per job, never stored').
    # GitHub: user x-access-token; GitLab: oauth2; Bitbucket: x-token-auth
    export GIT_CONFIG_VALUE_0="Authorization: Basic $(printf 'x-access-token:%s' "$GH_TOKEN" | base64 | tr -d '\n')"
    thub run --type sw \
      --git-repo https://github.com/yourorg/private-tests.git main \
      --env GIT_CONFIG_COUNT=1,GIT_CONFIG_KEY_0=http.extraHeader --env GIT_CONFIG_VALUE_0 \
      --command ./ci/test.sh --wait
  p.small.
    Don't put a token in the URL, #[code --git-options], #[code --command] or #[code --meta]: those are stored with the job
    and visible to anyone who can read it. Only #[code --env] values are masked.

  h3.h6 3. Clone options: ref, branch, tag, commit, depth
  +code('--git-repo <url> [<branch>|<tag>|<commit>] [--depth <n>]').
    --git-repo git@github.com:yourorg/tests.git                   # default branch, depth 1
    --git-repo git@github.com:yourorg/tests.git develop           # a branch
    --git-repo git@github.com:yourorg/tests.git v1.4.0            # a tag
    --git-repo git@github.com:yourorg/tests.git a1b2c3d           # a commit (may be abbreviated)
    --git-repo git@github.com:yourorg/tests.git main --depth 50   # last 50 commits
    --git-repo git@github.com:yourorg/tests.git main --depth 0    # full history (git describe, changelogs)
  table.table.table-sm.small
    tbody
      tr
        th Transports
        td #[code https://], #[code http://], #[code ssh://], #[code git://], #[code user@host:path]. #[code file://] and #[code ext::] are refused.
      tr
        th Default ref
        td The repository's default branch.
      tr
        th Depth
        td Default #[code 1]. #[code 0] = full history. If the server can't do shallow fetches (or the commit is abbreviated), the Client falls back to a full fetch.
      tr
        th Exact commit
        td Logged, and passed to the command as #[code $THUB_GIT_COMMIT]. The requested ref is #[code $JOB_GIT_BRANCH].
      tr
        th Extra git options
        td #[code --git-options] is inserted between #[code git] and its subcommand on every git call, e.g. #[code '-c http.sslVerify=false'] or #[code '-c core.sshCommand="…"'].
  +code('What the Client runs (see it with --dry-run)').
    git -C &lt;work> init -q
    git -C &lt;work> remote add origin &lt;url>
    git -C &lt;work> fetch -q --depth 1 origin &lt;ref>
    git -C &lt;work> checkout -q --detach FETCH_HEAD
    git -C &lt;work> rev-parse HEAD

  h3.h6 Submodules, LFS and cloning inside a container
  p.small.
    The Client checks out only the repository itself. Do anything more in the command, which runs in the checkout with the same #[code --env] variables:
  +code('Submodules / LFS').
    thub run --type sw --git-repo git@github.com:yourorg/tests.git main \
      --command 'git submodule update --init --recursive --depth 1 && git lfs pull && ./ci/test.sh' --wait
  +code('Clone yourself, e.g. inside a container (JOB_GIT_* carry the --git-repo values)').
    thub run --type sw --git-repo git@github.com:yourorg/tests.git main --depth 1 \
      --command 'docker run --rm -v "$HOME/.ssh:/root/.ssh:ro" -e JOB_GIT_REPO_URL -e JOB_GIT_BRANCH -e JOB_GIT_DEPTH \
                   --entrypoint sh alpine/git -c "git clone --depth \$JOB_GIT_DEPTH --branch \$JOB_GIT_BRANCH \$JOB_GIT_REPO_URL /src && ls /src"' \
      --wait
